DAY 4 - TYPE CONVERSION

1. TYPE CONVERSION

Converting one data type into another using:
int(), float(), str(), bool(), list(), tuple(), set(), dict()

2. IMPLICIT CASTING

Python automatically converts the data type when appropriate.

3. EXPLICIT CASTING

Programmer manually converts the data type using built-in functions.

4. INTEGER CONVERSION

int → float
int(2) → 2.0

int → str
str(2) → "2"

int → bool
bool(2) → True


5. FLOAT CONVERSION

float → int
int(3.1) → 3

float → str
str(3.1) → "3.1"

float → bool
bool(3.1) → True


6. STRING CONVERSION

x = "10"

int(x) → 10
float(x) → 10.0
bool(x) → True


7. BOOLEAN CONVERSION

int(True) → 1
int(False) → 0
float(True) → 1.0
str(False) → "False"


8. SEQUENCE CONVERSION

List → Tuple
tuple([10,20,30])

Tuple → List
list((10,20,30))

List → Set
set([10,20,20,30])
→ {10,20,30}

String → List
list("Python")

String → Tuple
tuple("Python")

String → Set
set("Python")


9. DICTIONARY CONVERSION

Dictionary requires key-value pairs.

data = [("name","Raju"), ("age",23)]
dict(data)


10. IMPORTANT

list(10), tuple(10), set(10)
→ TypeError because integer is not iterable.

{} → Empty dictionary
set() → Empty set